    "explanation": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">When the body is exposed to a pathogen for the first time, white blood cells take time to produce antibodies. After vaccination or an infection, some white blood cells become memory cells. If the same pathogen re-enters the body, these memory cells quickly produce the correct antibodies, often preventing infection.</p>",
